 @Astelch  That doesn't make any sense. All of the cost of a GPU is in R&D. Actually producing each physical chip is astronomically cheap in comparison. The more people buy it, and the more demand, the cheaper it gets, since the R&D gets distributed over more people.

In this case, it seems GPUs aren't legitimately getting that much better, just 5-10%. Consumers demand better and better cards, like 30-50% better, but chips aren't getting that much better. Instead, they're just making chips larger, use more power, and are giving them bigger and bigger heatsinks. Back in the day, you would use SLI with 2 cards, which would cost $1,000+ for each top of the line card and MOBO support. Now, they're just making one GPU have 2x GPUs worth of everything in it to push "top of the line" higher. SLI is discontinued.

Price/Performance does actually decrease generation-over-generation. They're just compensating for minimal Price/Performance gains by just making them more expensive, so that performance is still 40% gen over gen. No one wants to see 5% improvement over the last generation, so to maintain 40% performance increase year over year, 5% is in marginal tech improvements, 35% is in just paying for more GPU.

