 @sammiller6631  I estimated that the number of Tech YT watchers heavily skewed male based on analysis by another YTer on another video about his tech-related viewership--don't remember which one, sorry. Even if I'm wrong--and I'm sure Linus can simply look at the demographics of his views--and we assume 70% of viewers are male, that's still 5.3% of his viewership. At time of this response, that is 687k views on THIS video. That means 36,550 viewers could have difficulty in distinguishing what's happening in his charts and that number would grow with more viewers. Considering that this video has only been up for 7 hours, this is likely. I get that he wants to distinguish his charts--probably also red for AMD--but it makes it difficult for some viewers to see what was going on. If you are still annoyed by my estimation of demographics, then consider that 90% male viewership on a 1mil view video still means that 100k women are watching that video. I'm in no way trying to state that women don't enjoy tech videos but am pointing out that so many more men do.

The 8% of the male population having some form of colorblindness is taken straight off of wikipedia and the UK's NHS website that states colorblindness affects 1 in 12 males (read: 8.33%) and 1 in 200 females (read: 0.5%). Males are most affected because we have an X-Y chromosomal arrangement whereas women have an X-X arrangement. Red/green colorblindness is carried on the X-chromosome so men receive it from the mother where it is activated; women require both parents to give an affected X-chromosome for it to affect them and deem them colorblind, which shows the discrepancy of 8.33% for males vs. 0.5% for females. There are multiple types of colorblindness but the most common are a form of red/green colorblindness. So I rounded to 8% from the data given (yellow/blue colorblindness only affects 0.001% of either sex and mono/di-chromatic deficiencies are likewise smaller but not as insignificant). In fact, mono and di-chromatic would have an even tougher time seeing color contrasts between colors so it was probably on the more conservative side in terms of my rounding error.

